<p>Mandan, and North Dakota as a whole, experienced a significant turnaround from the Great Depression and Dust Bowl, driven by a booming wartime economy, federal construction projects, and the expansion of rural electrification. The decade also brought several major events to the city.</p>

<h3>1940</h3>

<p>In February, four Morton County nightclub operators were fined $25 for each count of violating Sabbath laws or operating a dance on Sunday. {% person "mike-guon" %}Mike Guon{% endperson %} was fined on both counts. {% person "joe-wetzstein" %}Joe Wetzstein{% endperson %}, {% person "charles-hawley" %}Charles Hawley{% endperson %}, and {% person "irvin-young" %}Irvin Young{% endperson %} were each fined for a single violation.</p>

<h3>1941</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1940s/259-1941-may-collins-courthouse-fire-koppy-mhsoc.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Forties - War and Post War Boom 1940-1949 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On May 15, the <a href="/places/gone-forever/collins-street-county-courthouse/">Morton County Courthouse</a>, built in 1885 on north Collins Avenue, was completely destroyed by fire. The blaze began at 5 p.m., when residents noticed smoke rolling from the upper windows and roof. County commissioners were still in session and initially unaware of the fire. Despite the city fire department's rapid response, it soon became apparent that the building could not be saved. Efforts focused on protecting the adjacent jail and sheriff's residence.</p>

<p>Aside from records stored in fireproof vaults, only two adding machines and a few law books were saved. The building was insured for $60,000 ($1,320,000 in 2025 dollars).</p>

<p>On August 5, 1941, Mandan recorded its second-highest daily temperature, 109°F, at 2 p.m. at the USDA Northern Great Plains Field Station weather bureau.</p>

<h3>1942</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1940s/250-cafe-ad-1942.gif" alt="Historic image from the The Forties - War and Post War Boom 1940-1949 page">
    <figcaption><em>Mandan Pioneer</em> newspaper ad, 1942</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Amid a surge of new café openings in early 1942, {% person "ernie-rober" %}Ernie Rober{% endperson %} and his wife opened the Uptown Cafe at 412 West Main Street, just west of the Lewis and Clark Hotel and across from the train depot. It occupied a new two-story tile-and-brick building constructed for the business and furnished with all-new equipment. The café seated 50 patrons, and {% person "brent-grant" %}Brent Grant{% endperson %} was the head chef. The building later housed a series of taverns and eateries, including the Roundup Bar and West Side Grill.</p>

<p>Despite federal calls to cancel state and county fairs to conserve tire rubber for the war effort, the Mandan Rodeo Association responded, "The show must go on," and held its Fourth of July event as scheduled. More than 20,000 paid admissions were already in hand for the three-day event. The parade drew its largest crowd to date under clear skies and a temperature of 75 degrees.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1940s/140-camp-map-normalized.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Forties - War and Post War Boom 1940-1949 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In late July, the last enrollees at the 260-member Veterans Civilian Conservation Corps camp were discharged after nearly eight years of continuous service. The camp opened in August 1934. Its workers initially served the State Park Service, erecting buildings and completing landscaping at Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park south of Mandan.</p>

<p><a href="/assets/documents/area-history/ccc-v2775-arley-olson-article.pdf">V2775 Camp Highlights</a> by {% person "arley-olson", "Arley Olson" %}A. Olson{% endperson %}</p>

<h3>1944</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1940s/708-sketch-vc2-victory-ship.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Forties - War and Post War Boom 1940-1949 page">
    <figcaption>Sketch of a VC2-class Victory ship</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On May 26, 1944, Mandan joined 218 American cities and towns honored with a Victory ship bearing its name. Victory ships were faster successors to Liberty ships. The <em>SS Mandan Victory</em>, a VC2-S-AP3 built by the Oregon Shipbuilding Corporation of Portland, Oregon, was launched for service with the U.S. Merchant Marine in support of the war effort. Victory ships were also named for Devils Lake and Dickinson.</p>

<h3>1945</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1940s/708-1945-photo-train-derailment-of-negro-soldiers.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Forties - War and Post War Boom 1940-1949 page">
    <figcaption>1945 Lyons troop-train derailment</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On February 20 at 5:20 p.m., an eastbound 12-car troop train carrying a battalion of approximately 300 African American soldiers derailed 2 miles west of Lyons, or 11 miles west of Mandan. Ten coaches left the tracks, injuring more than 100 people, five critically. Only the engine and rear two coaches remained upright. The injured were taken to area hospitals, while the troops were quartered in the Mandan Memorial Building, now City Hall. Fraine Barracks supplied bedding and other materials, meals were served in the Knights of Columbus Hall, and the Mandan Red Cross assisted with the response.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1940s/222-1945-war-ends.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Forties - War and Post War Boom 1940-1949 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Residents celebrated with the rest of the nation when World War II ended on August 14 with Japan's agreement in principle to unconditional surrender. More than 64,000 North Dakotans served in the armed forces during the war, while thousands more moved to cities to work in war-related industries. An estimated 700 men and women from Mandan served in the armed forces during this period.</p>

<p>The war also temporarily halted the construction of new electric lines, as materials were needed for the war effort. Following the war, the Rural Electrification Administration (REA) could focus its efforts to bring power to rural areas. Local cooperatives were formed, and construction began in earnest, with the first homes receiving power in 1946.</p>

<h3>1946</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1940s/229-1946-champ.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Forties - War and Post War Boom 1940-1949 page">
    <figcaption>Aeronca Champ</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In May, World War II veteran pilots {% person "jim-myers" %}Jim Myers{% endperson %} and {% person "richard-baron" %}Dick Baron{% endperson %} leased about 50 acres from {% person "blossom-lang-mcgillic", "Blossom Lang McGillic" %}Mrs. Blossom [Lang] McGillic{% endperson %}, 1.5 miles east of Mandan on the north side of Highway 10, and established the Missouri Valley Aviation School. They offered rides, flying lessons, charter service, and crop dusting. Aircraft based there included a new 1946 Aeronca Champ and a Taylorcraft BC-12 Traveler.</p>

<h3>1947</h3>

<p>Extreme cold winter weather in February caused a record demand for natural gas. Two schools were temporarily closed to reduce load. Montana-Dakota Utilities asked residents to cut the temperatures in their homes as much as possible to help their overburdened distribution system cope with the demand for natural gas.</p>

<h3>1949</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1940s/252-farmers-elevator.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Forties - War and Post War Boom 1940-1949 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Mandan's gleaming new white Farmers Union elevator at 211 East Main Street opened in late August for the fall harvest. {% person "l-f-zander" %}L. F. Zander{% endperson %} was president of the Farmers Union group; {% person "earl-schauss" %}Earl Schauss{% endperson %} was secretary-treasurer; and {% person "otto-hagerott" %}Otto Hagerott{% endperson %}, {% person "ted-hagerott" %}Ted Hagerott{% endperson %}, and {% person "elias-ellison" %}Elias Ellison{% endperson %} served on the board of directors. The elevator's first load of wheat came from the Zander farm. The first wheat received from the 1949 harvest came from the {% person "john-keller" %}John Keller{% endperson %} farm, weighed 60 pounds per bushel, and graded No. 1 Hard Dark Northern. Elevator manager {% person "john-calvin" %}John Calvin{% endperson %}, from Rawson in McKenzie County, temporarily lived with his wife and young son in a small trailer near the elevator. The elevator burned to the ground in the 1970s.</p>
